a it also happens to be a  native format for unity so there was  where we found some sort of common  ground between the two we constructed  the list of commands first and then  reformatted that into a JSON file that  then unity could directly consume and we  had to write a lot of code to kind of  glue those parts together but ultimately  it it worked fine like I said up here  it's it's the embedded graphics that  that were probably the biggest challenge  because we had to figure out how to  break those down basically into text and  what's what's called dehydrating a  graphics image and then rehydrating it  in other words rebuilding the graphic on  the other side in unity that was a that  was a tricky part to this whole thing  okay so alright so what did we do with  this so let's talk a little bit about  the animations that we built and I'll  show you the animations here in just a  minute the first one was offshore  logistics we were approached by a really  large oil company that wanted to drill  for oil in West Africa and in order to  do that you have to have an army almost  literally an army of ships and  helicopters moving personnel and moving  equipment and materials back and forth  from the shore out on the ocean to  these various drilling rigs and the  exact timing of when these boats arrive  and when the helicopters arrive is sort  of an intricate and very complex dance  that's very expensive by the way if you  could figure out a way to optimize those  routes and take say one boat out of the  system or one helicopter out of the  system each one of these these vessels  these work boats offshore rent for fifty  five thousand dollars per day so you can  just do the math on that and it's it's a  lot of money so a lot of our work was  trying to figure out exactly the cadence  of these deliveries for the boat so  let's let's take a look at what that's  like  so here is our unity portrayal I'll back  up just a little bit let's see I can't  see on the right-hand side 