y now and again  with sports teams that that we work with  and they're very interested in going  kind of beyond Moneyball how many of you  know the Moneyball story or I've seen  the movie or read the book well if you  have it your homework tonight is to go  home and rent that movie or read the  book because it's a it's a great book  about using essentially data science on  sports and and that'sthat's all terrific  you know looking at statistics looking  at the right metrics but going beyond  that and actually replaying certain game  situations in a realistic kind of scene  is going yet beyond Moneyball which is a  really exciting area manufacturing in  logistics is another sort of classic  application of this you could if you  could watch a supply chain or rip the  roof off of a factory and look inside it  and watch a day in the life of it it  gives you a really powerful way to kind  of look at how to improve it large-scale  infrastructure projects we're working  with the Qatari government on the the  soccer world cup in 2022 they've got  lots of big infrastructure ideas about  where to put hotels and soccer stadiums  and the like and working all of that out  we could do that with a lot of that with  unity and and certainly in teaching  almost anything  unity is a great platform so I'll just  kind of move really quickly in on some  of these there's there's those heads-up  displays are going to get a lot more  elaborate we're going to try to crack  the code around this direct architecture  idea do it using unity link and not only  controlling the actors on the stage but  what we'd also like to do is be able to  control the camera picture-in-picture  and swapping the two different you know  thing it's doing things with lighting  like spot putting spotlights on a  particular actor and watching that actor  through the scene and then finally if  you don't know about oculus rift I would  suggest you google it because that's a  sea change in virtual reality virtual  virtual reality headsets were in the  thousands of dollars up until very  recently the latest development kit that  they've released three hundred and fifty  dollars so what that's going to do is  going to bring virtual reality to the  mainstream and by the way unity supports  virtual reality so any of these games  can be done in a sort of a stereoscopic  sort of way and that's that's oculus  rift right there and that's the  stereoscopic scene that you see I so I  think there's lots of really exciting  possibilities when we we put Mathematica  together with other really powerful  things so we're going to just keep going  and see where it leads with that I'll  conclude thank you very much
 

